course: books
lesson: 81
title: Just So Stories (Rudyard Kipling), 6: The Sing-Song of Old Man Kangaroo
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Once the Kangaroo was a grey, woolly animal with four short legs and far too much pride, and he went to three made-up gods in turn, asking to be made "different from all other animals" by five o'clock that afternoon.
explain: The Little God Nqa and the Middle God Nquing both shout "Go away!", but the Big God Nqong says "Yes, I will!" and calls the always-hungry Yellow-Dog Dingo to chase him.
explain: The chase goes on all day across Australia, through deserts, mountains and grass; at a river with no bridge the Kangaroo stands up on his back legs and hops, and his hind legs grow longer and stronger with every bound.
explain: At five o'clock Nqong stops the race; the Kangaroo is now different from every other animal, and "run after" in two ways, but he is cross and hungry, and so is Dingo.
explain: It is told like a song, with lines that come round again and again ("He had to!"), and it ends with the two tired runners blaming each other: "That's your fault."
fact: The Kangaroo was "a Different Animal with four short legs. He was grey and he was woolly, and his pride was inordinate" ("inordinate" = far too much).
fact: He went to Nqa "at six before breakfast", to Nquing "at eight after breakfast", and to Nqong "at ten before dinner-time", who was sitting in his bath in the salt-pan; these gods are made up by Kipling for the story.
fact: Each time he asked for more: first "Make me different from all other animals", then also "wonderfully popular", then also "popular and wonderfully run after", all "by five this afternoon".
fact: Nqong called "Dingo--Yellow-Dog Dingo--always hungry, dusty in the sunshine", who said "What, that cat-rabbit?" and ran after him "grinning like a coal-scuttle".
fact: The joke: to be "run after" can mean to be popular, wanted by everybody, but Nqong makes it come true the other way: Dingo literally runs after him.
fact: The Kangaroo ran through "the desert", "the mountains", "the salt-pans", "the reed-beds", "the blue gums" (eucalyptus trees) and "the spinifex" (spiky grass), till his front legs ached.
fact: At "the Wollgong River" there was no bridge and no ferry-boat, "so he stood on his legs and hopped": "First he hopped one yard; then he hopped three yards; then he hopped five yards; his legs growing stronger; his legs growing longer."
fact: He hopped "like a cricket; like a pea in a saucepan; or a new rubber ball on a nursery floor", and "stuck out his tail for a balance-weight behind him".
fact: At five o'clock the Kangaroo sat with his tail "like a milking-stool behind him" and said "Thank goodness that's finished!"; Dingo, now "Tired-Dog Dingo" and "Poor Dog Dingo", sat down, hung out his tongue and howled, and wanted something for his tea.
fact: He complained that Dingo had "altered my shape so I'll never get it back; and he's played Old Scratch with my legs" ("Old Scratch" = an old name for the Devil, so "played Old Scratch with" = ruined).
fact: Nqong teased him: "Why aren't you grateful to Yellow-Dog Dingo?" The Kangaroo called it "a practical joke" (a trick played on someone), then quickly said sorry when Nqong threatened to whistle up Dingo and "run your hind legs off"; Nqong said "Come and ask me about it tomorrow, because I'm going to wash", and went, and Kangaroo and Dingo "each said, 'That's your fault.'"
fact: The song at the end calls him "a Boomer" (Australian for a big male kangaroo), says his back legs worked "like pistons", "Twenty-five feet to a bound", and that they ran "From Torres Straits to the Leeuwin", the far north and far south-west corners of Australia.
fact: Real biology: kangaroos are marsupials, carrying their babies (joeys) in a pouch; their long back legs have springy tendons that store energy, so hopping is a very cheap way to travel far.
fact: Real biology: a big red kangaroo can cover about eight metres (around twenty-five feet) in one bound, so Kipling's number is about right; the tail balances it when hopping and props it like a fifth leg when it moves slowly.
fact: Real biology: dingoes are Australia's wild dogs, which came with people thousands of years ago and really do hunt kangaroos; but kangaroos got their long legs over millions of years, not in one afternoon's chase.
exercise: What was the Kangaroo like before the story? | A grey, woolly animal with four short legs | A tall animal with long back legs and a tail | A sandy-yellow cat-shaped beast of the veldt | A long-necked animal with a hump on his back
exercise: What does "his pride was inordinate" mean? | He was far too proud | He was very shy | He was quite ordinary | He was very hungry
exercise: Whom did he go to first, at six before breakfast? | The Little God Nqa | The Middle God Nquing | The Big God Nqong | Yellow-Dog Dingo
exercise: What did Nqa and Nquing both shout? | "Go away!" | "Yes, I will!" | "Humph!" | "Hush!"
exercise: When did he go to the Middle God Nquing? | At eight after breakfast | At six before breakfast | At ten before dinner-time | At five in the afternoon
exercise: Where was Nqong when the Kangaroo came? | In his bath in the salt-pan | On a sandflat by the sea | In a burrow in the spinifex | On top of a tall blue gum
exercise: What did the Kangaroo ask Nqong for? | To be different, popular and wonderfully run after by five | To be given long legs and a tail by the end of breakfast | To be made king of all the animals in the middle of Australia | To be left alone and never to be chased by anyone at all
exercise: Whom did Nqong call to make the Kangaroo "run after"? | Yellow-Dog Dingo | The Kolokolo Bird | The Python-Rock-Snake | The Djinn of All Deserts
exercise: What did Dingo call the Kangaroo? | "That cat-rabbit" | "That woolly sheep" | "That proud bunny" | "That grey goat"
exercise: How did the Kangaroo run at first? | On his four little legs like a bunny | On his two long hind legs like today | Rolling along in a cloud of dust | Swimming down the Wollgong River
exercise: What does "run after" mean in the joke? | Both "very popular" and "chased" | Both "running fast" and "late" | Both "very hungry" and "tired out" | Both "proud" and "woolly"
exercise: Which of these did the Kangaroo NOT run through? | A shadowy forest of speckled trees | The salt-pans | The reed-beds | The spiky spinifex grass of the desert
exercise: What are "blue gums"? | A kind of eucalyptus tree | Sweets that turn your tongue blue | A river in the middle of Australia | Blue rocks in the salt-pans
exercise: Why did the Kangaroo start to hop? | He came to a river with no bridge or ferry-boat | Nqong shouted at him to hop instead of running | Dingo bit his front legs and he could not run | He saw a cricket hopping and wanted to copy it
exercise: How did his hops grow? | One yard, then three yards, then five yards | Five yards, then three yards, then one yard | One yard, then two yards, then three yards | Ten yards at once, then twenty, then thirty
exercise: What did he use as a balance-weight when hopping? | His tail | His front legs | His long ears | His woolly fur
exercise: Which is NOT one of the things he hopped like? | A Whale on the shingle | A little cricket in the grass | A pea in a saucepan | A new rubber ball
exercise: Who stopped the race, and at what time? | Nqong, at five o'clock | Dingo, at tea-time | Nqa, at six o'clock | The Kangaroo himself, at noon
exercise: What did the Kangaroo say when he sat down? | "Thank goodness that's finished!" | "Thank you, Yellow-Dog Dingo!" | "Please make me different once more!" | "Now I am popular at last!"
exercise: What does "played Old Scratch with my legs" mean? | Ruined my legs | Scratched my legs | Made my legs itch | Played games on my legs
exercise: What did the Kangaroo call what Nqong had done? | A practical joke | A great piece of magic | A kind, generous gift | A fair, honest race
exercise: What did Nqong threaten to do when the Kangaroo said "Joke"? | Whistle up Dingo and run his hind legs off | Turn him back into a grey, woolly animal | Throw him into the salt-pan for a bath | Give him a hump like the Camel's for being rude
exercise: What did Dingo want at the end? | Something for his tea | A long rest in the shade | A bath in the salt-pan | A new pair of fast legs
exercise: What did Kangaroo and Dingo each say at the very end? | "That's your fault." | "Thank goodness that's finished!" | "Come and ask me tomorrow." | "Go away!"
exercise: What is a "Boomer" in the song? | A big male kangaroo | A loud noise | A kind of wild dog | A river boat
exercise: Real biology: what are kangaroos? | Marsupials that carry their babies in a pouch | Big rabbits that have learned to stand up tall | A kind of deer that lives only in the desert | Reptiles that lay their eggs in the hot sand
exercise: Real biology: how do kangaroos use their tail when moving slowly? | As a fifth leg to lean on | As a whip against dingoes | To steer like a boat's rudder | They hold it up off the ground
exercise: Real biology: how did kangaroos really get long back legs? | Slowly, over millions of years | From one long chase by a dingo | By stretching them in a river | From magic by a god called Nqong
test: Isla says: "Kangaroo asked Nqong to be chased by a dog, so it's fair." Put her right. | He asked to be popular and "run after", not chased | He asked to be chased only on Sunday afternoons | He asked Nqong for a dog to keep as a friendly pet | He asked Nqong to make Dingo stop being so hungry
test: Brody, Aussie: "Kangaroo ran from mornin' till the arvo, flat out like a lizard drinkin'." ("flat out like a lizard drinking" = very busy, going hard.) When did it end? | Five o'clock | Ten before dinner | Six before breakfast | Just before midnight
test: What would have happened if there had been a bridge over the Wollgong River? | He might have kept running on four legs instead of hopping | Dingo would have fallen into the river and given up | Nqong would have stopped the race much earlier on | The Kangaroo would have swum across like a Whale
test: True or false: at the end the Kangaroo is happy with his new shape. | False: he moans that his shape and legs are spoiled | True: he thanks Dingo warmly for all his hard work | True: he dances on a sandbank to show off his legs | False: he asks Nqong to give him four short legs again
test: Tyler, Gen Z: "Kangaroo wanted to be famous and got chased by a dog lmao, be careful what you wish for fr." ("fr" = for real.) Which earlier animal also got a body change he did not like because of his own words? | The Camel, who said "Humph!" once too often | The Leopard, who asked for little spots | The Whale, who ate the shipwrecked Mariner | The Elephant's Child, who said "Thank you"
test: Sarge, rough: "Stop whinging, you got what you asked for, now shift." What did the Kangaroo really ask for? | To be different, popular and run after by five | To be left alone in the middle of Australia | To be fast enough to escape from Dingo | To be given a tail as a balance-weight
test: Compare: the Elephant's Child's trunk and the Kangaroo's legs were both made by pulling or running. Which is true? | Both were stretched by a hard struggle, and both turned out useful | Both were given by a magic Djinn who lived in the desert | Both came from eating stale cake-crumbs on a beach | Both were punishments for being idle with the world so new
test: In which TWO stories so far did the animal ask for his change himself? | The Leopard and Old Man Kangaroo | The Whale and the Camel | The Rhinoceros and the Camel | The Whale and the Elephant's Child
test: Fiona, Scotland: "Yon Dingo's a greetin' wee dug, hingin' oot his tongue." ("greetin'" = crying, "dug" = dog.) What did Dingo do at five o'clock? | Sat down, hung out his tongue and howled | Caught the Kangaroo and ate him for tea | Ran off home to his mother in the hills | Thanked Nqong and lay down in the bath
test: In the Camel story a Djinn makes the hump; in this story a god makes the change. What does each one tell the animal about the change? | The Djinn: it lets him work three days without eating; Nqong: come and ask tomorrow | The Djinn: go away at once; Nqong: yes, I will | The Djinn: thank goodness it's finished; Nqong: it's your fault | The Djinn: hush; Nqong: come hither, Little One
test: Mason, Gen Alpha: "Nqong was lowkey trolling the Kangaroo, no cap." ("trolling" = teasing someone on purpose.) Which line shows Nqong teasing? | "Why aren't you grateful to Yellow-Dog Dingo?" | "Make me different from all other animals" | "Thank goodness that's finished!" | "What, that cat-rabbit?"
test: The Whale story had "Fifty North and Forty West"; this song has "From Torres Straits to the Leeuwin". What are these? | Real places you can find in an atlas | Magic words that make animals change | Names of Kipling's own children | Names of ships in the stories
test: Across the six stories, which animal's change is the only one that came from Kipling's idea of hiding in shadows? | The Giraffe and the Zebra, in the Leopard story | The Kangaroo, in the Sing-Song | The Rhino, by the Red Sea | The Whale, at Fifty North
test: ★★★ The Kangaroo's hops went one yard, three yards, five yards. If the pattern went on, how long would the next hop be? | Seven yards | Six yards | Eight yards | Nine yards
test: ★★★ The chase began soon after ten o'clock and Nqong stopped it at five o'clock. About how many hours did it last? | About seven hours | About five hours | About fifteen hours | About three hours
test: ★★★ Trap: Dingo is called "Yellow-Dog Dingo" most of the time. What two other names does he get when he is worn out at the end? | Tired-Dog Dingo and Poor Dog Dingo | Old Man Dingo and Big Dog Dingo | Hungry Dingo and Grey Dog Dingo | Mad Dog Dingo and Wild Dog Dingo
exam: Identify the deity who acceded to the Kangaroo's request, together with the hour at which the request was made. | The Big God Nqong, at ten before dinner-time | The Little God Nqa, at six before breakfast | The Middle God Nquing, at eight after breakfast | The Big God Nqong, at five in the afternoon
exam: Ascertain the circumstance which first compelled the Kangaroo to proceed upon his hind legs alone. | The absence of any bridge or ferry across the Wollgong River | The express instruction of Nqong delivered from his bath | The exhaustion of his front legs in the blue gums | The injury of his front legs by the teeth of Dingo
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the double meaning upon which the narrative turns. | "Run after" signifies both popularity and literal pursuit | "Old Man" signifies both great age and great wisdom | "Different" signifies both strange and very clever | "Boomer" signifies both a loud noise and a river
exam: State, in accordance with established zoology, the classification of the kangaroo. | A marsupial, bearing its young in a pouch | A rodent, related to the rabbit and the hare | A reptile, laying its eggs in warm sand | A deer, inhabiting the deserts of Asia
